1054
01:01:58,453 --> 01:02:00,281
And I wouldn’t blame him.

1055
01:02:00,368 --> 01:02:04,024
But once this is all mine,

1056
01:02:04,111 --> 01:02:07,636
he will still be a
thorn in my side.

1057
01:02:07,724 --> 01:02:11,249
And his mother falling
into line won’t help.

1058
01:02:11,336 --> 01:02:13,251
No.

1059
01:02:13,338 --> 01:02:17,168
He’s going to require
a far stronger hand.

1060
01:02:17,255 --> 01:02:19,083
It’s not a matter of if.

1061
01:02:19,170 --> 01:02:22,477
Simply put, the question
becomes do I take care

1062
01:02:22,564 --> 01:02:26,699
of Telemachus now, or do
I take care of him later?

1063
01:02:30,529 --> 01:02:33,271
It’s like my father used to say.

1064
01:02:33,358 --> 01:02:37,579
Only a fool leaves
today’s jobs until tomorrow.

1065
01:02:37,666 --> 01:02:42,802
Well, Antinous is no fool.

1066
01:02:46,197 --> 01:02:47,807
Neither will this loyal dog.

1067
01:02:54,205 --> 01:02:56,120
No.

1068
01:02:56,207 --> 01:02:58,209
This loyal dog will be
ready for you, Antinous.

1069
01:03:06,783 --> 01:03:09,089
My reward...

1070
01:03:09,176 --> 01:03:10,047
Speak to me.

1071
01:03:11,875 --> 01:03:13,659
Odysseus!
I need to get my rewards!

1072
01:03:13,746 --> 01:03:15,356
What?
What are you doing?

1073
01:03:15,443 --> 01:03:17,881
My rewards, my gold!
They’re speaking to me!

1074
01:03:17,968 --> 01:03:19,447
- Not when we’re so close!
- Yes!

1075
01:03:19,534 --> 01:03:21,580
No! Just pass the rocks,
and we’re nearly home!

1076
01:03:21,667 --> 01:03:22,929
They speak to me!

1077
01:03:23,016 --> 01:03:24,670
Polites!
Polites!

1078
01:03:24,757 --> 01:03:26,411
Wake up!

1079
01:03:26,498 --> 01:03:27,847
Take this beeswax!

1080
01:03:27,934 --> 01:03:28,848
Put it in your ears!

1081
01:03:28,935 --> 01:03:30,763
We must block their song!

1082
01:03:30,850 --> 01:03:33,374
We must block
the siren’s song!

1083
01:03:33,461 --> 01:03:34,419
Block it!

1084
01:03:34,506 --> 01:03:36,551
We cannot hear them!

1085
01:03:36,638 --> 01:03:37,901
We’ve got to stay clear of them!

1086
01:03:37,988 --> 01:03:39,163
We’ve got to stay away!

1087
01:03:39,250 --> 01:03:40,555
Steady on the course...

1088
01:03:40,642 --> 01:03:42,819
until we can’t hear them!

1089
01:03:42,906 --> 01:03:44,211
Steer away...

1090
01:03:44,298 --> 01:03:45,212
Steer away.

1091
01:03:52,393 --> 01:03:54,308
That song...
That song...

1092
01:03:56,049 --> 01:03:57,529
Oh...

1093
01:03:57,616 --> 01:03:59,052
I want to hear that song.

1094
01:04:00,358 --> 01:04:01,663
Odysseus!

1095
01:04:05,276 --> 01:04:07,495
- That song...
- Odysseus!

1096
01:04:07,582 --> 01:04:09,715
Odysseus!
Don’t listen to them!

1097
01:04:09,802 --> 01:04:11,021
Here!

1098
01:04:11,108 --> 01:04:12,892
No, the wax!

1099
01:04:12,979 --> 01:04:13,850
The wax!

1100
01:04:14,981 --> 01:04:16,722
The song...

1101
01:04:16,809 --> 01:04:20,552
Odysseus! We’re leaving!
We’re getting out of here!

1102
01:04:20,639 --> 01:04:22,032
I want to hear that song.

1103
01:04:24,121 --> 01:04:25,383
They know nothing but death!

1104
01:04:25,470 --> 01:04:27,211
- They want to help us.
- They do not.

1105
01:04:27,298 --> 01:04:29,387
- They do want to help us.
- They don’t want to help us.

1106
01:04:29,474 --> 01:04:31,171
- Polites...
- Think of your family!

1107
01:04:31,258 --> 01:04:33,304
- Polites, they want to help us.
- No, no, no!

1108
01:04:33,391 --> 01:04:34,522
They will not!

1109
01:04:34,609 --> 01:04:35,697
Polites, they know
how to get home.

1110
01:04:35,784 --> 01:04:37,134
We’ve come this far!

1111
01:04:37,221 --> 01:04:38,570
- Polites...
- We’ve come this far!

1112
01:04:38,657 --> 01:04:39,658
- They only know death!
- They have wisdom!

1113
01:04:39,745 --> 01:04:40,746
They only know death!

1114
01:04:42,182 --> 01:04:43,140
Their song is wisdom.

1115
01:04:43,227 --> 01:04:45,229
Hey! Snap out of it!

1116
01:04:45,316 --> 01:04:46,534
They want to help us.

1117
01:04:46,621 --> 01:04:48,623
They know what
lies beneath the waves.

1118
01:04:48,710 --> 01:04:49,755
I must hear this song.

1119
01:04:51,409 --> 01:04:53,237
Song of the sirens.

1120
01:04:53,324 --> 01:04:55,326
What a beautiful song.

1121
01:04:55,413 --> 01:04:56,588
Huh.

1122
01:05:00,984 --> 01:05:02,507
They want to help us get home.

1123
01:05:04,770 --> 01:05:06,728
They do.

1124
01:05:06,815 --> 01:05:07,729
They have knowledge.

1125
01:05:07,816 --> 01:05:10,907
Polites, they have wisdom.

1126
01:05:12,299 --> 01:05:13,561
- Odysseus...
- Their beautiful song.

1127
01:05:13,648 --> 01:05:15,041
I must hear it.

1128
01:05:15,128 --> 01:05:17,130
Odysseus, they know
nothing but death.

1129
01:05:19,437 --> 01:05:20,786
They have knowledge.

1130
01:05:20,873 --> 01:05:22,440
They know nothing but death.

1131
01:05:22,527 --> 01:05:23,615
Think of home.

1132
01:05:26,618 --> 01:05:28,489
The song.

1133
01:05:28,576 --> 01:05:31,014
No, Polites, we
must steer towards them.

1134
01:05:31,101 --> 01:05:32,058
Polites, they want to help us!

1135
01:05:32,145 --> 01:05:33,364
No, they don’t!

1136
01:05:33,451 --> 01:05:35,714
Yes!
Their song!

1137
01:05:38,717 --> 01:05:39,761
I want to hear their song...

1138
01:05:46,551 --> 01:05:49,510
Please, Polites.
They sing for me!

1139
01:05:49,597 --> 01:05:51,034
Odysseus!

1140
01:05:51,121 --> 01:05:53,340
Odysseus!
Hear me, Odysseus!

1141
01:05:55,516 --> 01:05:58,998
You must not listen
to them, Odysseus!

1142
01:05:59,085 --> 01:06:00,652
I can only hear their song.

1143
01:06:00,739 --> 01:06:02,175
Odysseus!

1144
01:06:02,262 --> 01:06:03,916
No!

1145
01:06:04,003 --> 01:06:05,439
You can’t hear me
over their song!

1146
01:06:06,527 --> 01:06:08,268
Odysseus!

1147
01:06:08,355 --> 01:06:11,445
You will die if you don’t
make it past these rocks!

1148
01:06:11,532 --> 01:06:16,276
And if you cannot hear me,
I cannot help you!

1149
01:06:16,363 --> 01:06:17,756
I cannot help you.

1150
01:06:20,106 --> 01:06:21,412
Odysseus!

1151
01:06:21,499 --> 01:06:22,804
Odysseus, no!

1152
01:06:26,765 --> 01:06:29,811
Polites, we must
steer away from the rocks.

1153
01:06:29,898 --> 01:06:31,335
Please steer away from them.

1154
01:06:35,774 --> 01:06:37,602
We must get to Ithaca.

1155
01:06:37,689 --> 01:06:39,038
They will give us
a hero’s welcome.

1156
01:06:39,125 --> 01:06:40,605
A king’s welcome.

1157
01:06:44,522 --> 01:06:46,176
Damn the sirens.

1158
01:06:46,263 --> 01:06:48,656
And damn these seas, too.

1159
01:06:48,743 --> 01:06:50,963
Polites, please forgive me.

1160
01:06:51,050 --> 01:06:53,922
You have saved
both of our lives.

1161
01:06:54,010 --> 01:06:56,273
It was an honor, my king.

1162
01:06:56,360 --> 01:06:58,623
But now our course is set.

1163
01:06:58,710 --> 01:07:00,538
You are almost home.

1164
01:07:00,625 --> 01:07:02,148
And that is your reward.

1165
01:07:03,802 --> 01:07:07,936
But mind, my king,
my reward is treasure.

1166
01:07:08,024 --> 01:07:10,330
You must listen to me.
You don’t understand.

1167
01:07:10,417 --> 01:07:12,680
No, no, no, I do understand.

1168
01:07:12,767 --> 01:07:15,118
You have a gift from the gods.

1169
01:07:15,205 --> 01:07:16,815
And Ithaca, now
that is your reward.

1170
01:07:16,902 --> 01:07:17,903
Now I am in pursuit of mine.

1171
01:07:17,990 --> 01:07:20,558
You said, your words.

1172
01:07:20,645 --> 01:07:22,125
What do you mean?

1173
01:07:22,212 --> 01:07:23,822
Gifts for good men.
Did you not?

1174
01:07:23,909 --> 01:07:25,693
Please, you do not understand.

1175
01:07:25,780 --> 01:07:28,783
No, I do understand. It’s you
that doesn’t understand.

1176
01:07:28,870 --> 01:07:30,829
I saw Athena
herself give it to you.

1177
01:07:30,916 --> 01:07:32,657
- I saw her.
- No, no.

1178
01:07:32,744 --> 01:07:34,050
A gift from the gods. Reward.

1179
01:07:34,137 --> 01:07:35,573
No, it’s not a gift.

1180
01:07:35,660 --> 01:07:37,444
- Oh, it’s not a gift?
- No, it’s not.

1181
01:07:37,531 --> 01:07:38,793
I see it.

1182
01:07:38,880 --> 01:07:41,013
I see, clever Polites,
loyal Polites,

1183
01:07:41,100 --> 01:07:42,667
worthy of scars Polites,

1184
01:07:42,754 --> 01:07:45,626
but not worthy of a gift
from the gods, is that it?

1185
01:07:45,713 --> 01:07:47,889
Please listen to me.
Please listen to me.

1186
01:07:47,976 --> 01:07:49,674
Exactly like Troy, is it?

1187
01:07:49,761 --> 01:07:52,068
Exactly like Troy.

1188
01:07:52,155 --> 01:07:53,460
I am going to take
what is owed to me.

1189
01:07:53,547 --> 01:07:54,983
For good men.

1190
01:07:55,071 --> 01:07:58,726
No, no, do not take that.
Do not take that!

1191
01:07:58,813 --> 01:08:00,424
Bring it back.
You do not understand.

1192
01:08:00,511 --> 01:08:02,295
- No, stop.
- I do understand.

1193
01:08:02,382 --> 01:08:04,863
Stop!
Polites, please, please.

1194
01:08:04,950 --> 01:08:08,519
My King, I am only opening this

1195
01:08:08,606 --> 01:08:09,737
because this is
what is owed to me.

1196
01:08:09,824 --> 01:08:11,087
- No.
- Yes.

1197
01:08:22,576 --> 01:08:23,751
Athena.

1198
01:08:25,318 --> 01:08:27,451
Athena, please.

1199
01:08:57,307 --> 01:09:01,137
Perhaps the fates are more
generous than we thought.

1200
01:09:01,224 --> 01:09:02,747
Athena.

1201
01:09:02,834 --> 01:09:05,141
Even the winds had mercy
on you and brought you home.

1202
01:09:07,534 --> 01:09:09,188
Reward. Reward.

1203
01:09:12,452 --> 01:09:17,501
Great Odysseus finally returned
to the shores of Ithaca.

1204
01:09:18,850 --> 01:09:20,156
Ithaca.

1205
01:09:20,243 --> 01:09:24,464
Telemachus...
Penelope!

1206
01:09:24,551 --> 01:09:27,424
The great Odysseus...

1207
01:09:27,511 --> 01:09:29,730
At the end of his ropes.

1208
01:09:31,906 --> 01:09:33,778
No.

1209
01:09:33,865 --> 01:09:34,953
Not yet.
Not quite.

1210
01:09:36,607 --> 01:09:38,652
Look.

1211
01:09:38,739 --> 01:09:40,045
There, Your Majesty.

1212
01:09:46,312 --> 01:09:49,185
Your people are in panic.

1213
01:09:49,272 --> 01:09:51,752
Perhaps there is no more
glory left for you here.

1214
01:09:54,364 --> 01:09:58,063
Penelope and Telemachus
are doing all they can.

1215
01:09:58,150 --> 01:10:01,022
But your city is
being taken by force.

1216
01:10:01,109 --> 01:10:03,590
My world burns.

1217
01:10:03,677 --> 01:10:07,203
And the gods abandon me
with my hands tied.

1218
01:10:07,290 --> 01:10:09,379
I can offer no more aid,
and you know this.

1219
01:10:13,774 --> 01:10:16,516
By breathing wind
into your sails,

1220
01:10:16,603 --> 01:10:20,128
strategizing your
boat as a weapon,

1221
01:10:20,216 --> 01:10:22,783
and giving you a bag of wind,

1222
01:10:22,870 --> 01:10:26,961
your three gifts have been used.

1223
01:10:27,048 --> 01:10:30,704
But great goddess,
I never opened the bag of winds.

1224
01:10:30,791 --> 01:10:34,273
It was Polites.
Polites opened the bag of winds.

1225
01:10:34,360 --> 01:10:36,144
Not I.

1226
01:10:36,232 --> 01:10:37,450
A wrinkle.

1227
01:10:39,452 --> 01:10:43,630
Perhaps it is enough
for the gods to allow me

1228
01:10:43,717 --> 01:10:47,895
to aid you one last
time on your journey.

1229
01:10:47,982 --> 01:10:51,551
I have one boon.
One gift left.

1230
01:10:51,638 --> 01:10:53,901
I just pray I don’t
have to use it.

1231
01:10:53,988 --> 01:10:58,036
Yes, but you cannot
just go home, Odysseus.

1232
01:10:58,123 --> 01:11:00,734
Not anymore.

1233
01:11:00,821 --> 01:11:04,216
The people of Ithaca,
they are a mob.

1234
01:11:04,303 --> 01:11:06,871
They run through the
streets hungry for blood.

1235
01:11:06,958 --> 01:11:08,916
They would have yours.

1236
01:11:09,003 --> 01:11:11,136
You will not make it past them.

1237
01:11:11,223 --> 01:11:14,008
You will not even make it
to your front door.

1238
01:11:14,095 --> 01:11:18,186
And if you did, the suitors
would be waiting for you.

1239
01:11:20,145 --> 01:11:22,495
In disguise.

1240
01:11:22,582 --> 01:11:24,976
In disguise to hide
my face in my own kingdom.

1241
01:11:25,063 --> 01:11:26,804
I shall dress as a
wandering beggar

1242
01:11:26,891 --> 01:11:28,414
and learn how things stand.

1243
01:11:28,501 --> 01:11:31,461
What will you do,
Great Odysseus?

1244
01:11:31,548 --> 01:11:32,940
I will defend my home.

1245
01:11:33,027 --> 01:11:34,551
My family.

1246
01:11:34,638 --> 01:11:36,770
I must stop this
before it’s too late.

1247
01:11:36,857 --> 01:11:38,772
Or even if it’s as you say,

1248
01:11:38,859 --> 01:11:41,949
there’s no glory
left for either of us.

1249
01:11:43,821 --> 01:11:46,214
Finish the game, Odysseus.

1250
01:11:46,302 --> 01:11:48,391
And finish it well.

1251
01:11:48,478 --> 01:11:52,569
For not all gods
have abandoned you.

1252
01:11:52,656 --> 01:11:55,180
And Poseidon still
thirsts for revenge.

1253
01:12:04,537 --> 01:12:06,626
We must do something, Mother.

1254
01:12:06,713 --> 01:12:08,280
No.

1255
01:12:08,367 --> 01:12:09,716
I will not let them kill you.

1256
01:12:09,803 --> 01:12:11,501
You must get
control of the city.

1257
01:12:11,588 --> 01:12:15,069
We will wait until
your father returns.

1258
01:12:15,156 --> 01:12:16,462
Rioting!

1259
01:12:16,549 --> 01:12:18,464
Looting and violence,

1260
01:12:18,551 --> 01:12:21,249
and you want to wait for
Odysseus to return?

1261
01:12:23,251 --> 01:12:25,384
Do you still think
you’ll have a kingdom by then?

1262
01:12:29,736 --> 01:12:33,653
Without a king,
my queen,

1263
01:12:33,740 --> 01:12:38,789
the city succumbs to
its more primal urges.

1264
01:12:38,876 --> 01:12:42,575
I will take your hand
and the crown with it.

1265
01:12:42,662 --> 01:12:47,406
And all will be well
when King Antinous reigns.

1266
01:12:48,842 --> 01:12:51,236
It is time, my lord.

1267
01:12:51,323 --> 01:12:54,370
Time enough indeed
for the final challenge.

1268
01:12:57,721 --> 01:13:01,289
No more games, loyal widow.

1269
01:13:01,377 --> 01:13:03,683
Being king is no game.

1270
01:13:03,770 --> 01:13:05,206
And only the worthy
wear our crown

1271
01:13:05,293 --> 01:13:06,947
and complete this final task.

1272
01:13:09,689 --> 01:13:13,780
Only our king can fire an
arrow through those axe blades.

1273
01:13:13,867 --> 01:13:16,827
And only our king has done it.

1274
01:13:16,914 --> 01:13:21,092
So are you our king, Antinous?

1275
01:13:21,179 --> 01:13:26,793
Or does Odysseus, even in
his absence, carry the crown?

1276
01:13:26,880 --> 01:13:29,622
You’re not worth of such
a great feat!

1277
01:13:29,709 --> 01:13:31,189
I challenge you for
my Father’s kingdom!

1278
01:13:31,276 --> 01:13:32,799
Snake in the grass!

1279
01:13:35,280 --> 01:13:37,064
Stay in your place, pup!

1280
01:13:37,151 --> 01:13:39,153
Telemachus!

1281
01:13:39,240 --> 01:13:43,201
But if I must, I will
teach you to keep your place!

1282
01:13:44,898 --> 01:13:47,292
The crown will never be yours!

1283
01:13:49,773 --> 01:13:51,296
Snake in the grass!

1284
01:13:57,824 --> 01:14:00,218
Interloper! Villain!

1285
01:14:00,305 --> 01:14:01,872
Yes, I am.

1286
01:14:03,395 --> 01:14:06,224
And much worse.

1287
01:14:06,311 --> 01:14:08,008
Begging your apologies.

1288
01:14:08,095 --> 01:14:11,229
I was merely returning
the young man his sword.

1289
01:14:14,754 --> 01:14:16,713
I...

1290
01:14:16,800 --> 01:14:20,368
I would like to ask the young
lady if I may compete.

1291
01:14:20,456 --> 01:14:21,457
Another suitor?

1292
01:14:24,634 --> 01:14:26,200
With the queen’s
permission, of course.

1293
01:14:32,555 --> 01:14:34,513
We need no more suitors!

1294
01:14:34,600 --> 01:14:35,862
I will--

1295
01:14:35,949 --> 01:14:37,211
The courtesy of
my husband’s house

1296
01:14:37,298 --> 01:14:38,691
is much diminished
as of late.

1297
01:14:42,739 --> 01:14:44,697
You may compete.

1298
01:14:44,784 --> 01:14:47,787
And I pray you might
restore what has been lost.

1299
01:14:47,874 --> 01:14:51,922
Only Odysseus can put an
arrow through those axe blades.

1300
01:14:52,009 --> 01:14:56,100
So, let’s see how good you are.

1301
01:14:56,187 --> 01:14:57,101
Good.

1302
01:14:59,756 --> 01:15:02,323
Clever.

1303
01:15:02,410 --> 01:15:03,716
Clever is better than good.

1304
01:15:15,380 --> 01:15:17,469
It’s a farce!

1305
01:15:17,556 --> 01:15:20,211
He can’t even keep the
arrow straight, my queen.

1306
01:15:21,865 --> 01:15:24,868
Best keep your eye
on the target, old man.

1307
01:15:27,044 --> 01:15:30,438
So, only Odysseus
himself can make this shot.

1308
01:15:48,326 --> 01:15:50,850
And I must be Odysseus.

1309
01:15:50,937 --> 01:15:53,070
But Odysseus is dead!

1310
01:15:53,157 --> 01:15:55,159
- No!
- Father!

1311
01:15:57,770 --> 01:16:01,557
You have overstayed your
welcome in my house too long!

1312
01:16:01,644 --> 01:16:02,688
Antinous!

1313
01:16:04,734 --> 01:16:06,518
You can leave on
your own accord.

1314
01:16:06,605 --> 01:16:08,041
Never!

1315
01:16:08,128 --> 01:16:11,088
This kingdom is mine!

1316
01:16:11,175 --> 01:16:12,916
Or you can go another way!

1317
01:16:19,662 --> 01:16:23,666
Death is too good for you.

1318
01:16:23,753 --> 01:16:25,189
Watch your tongue!

1319
01:16:25,276 --> 01:16:27,887
Or we will take it from you.

1320
01:16:27,974 --> 01:16:29,149
No!

1321
01:16:36,026 --> 01:16:37,201
My gods!

1322
01:16:38,898 --> 01:16:40,117
Help me!

1323
01:16:40,204 --> 01:16:42,075
Have mercy on me!

1324
01:16:58,526 --> 01:17:00,006
Here I am, monster!

1325
01:17:05,098 --> 01:17:09,015
I’ll slaughter you and
your family,

1326
01:17:09,102 --> 01:17:11,583
and the whole of Ithaca!

1327
01:17:11,670 --> 01:17:13,019
No! No!

1328
01:17:13,106 --> 01:17:14,673
Here I am! Here I am!

1329
01:17:14,760 --> 01:17:16,370
I’m the one you want!

1330
01:17:16,457 --> 01:17:18,416
Take me! Take me!

1331
01:17:20,113 --> 01:17:21,201
Father!

1332
01:17:22,768 --> 01:17:23,769
Father!

1333
01:17:46,357 --> 01:17:47,358
Odysseus?

1334
01:17:49,142 --> 01:17:50,013
Odysseus!

1335
01:17:51,667 --> 01:17:53,059
No!

1336
01:17:53,146 --> 01:17:55,192
What did he say to you, son?

1337
01:17:55,279 --> 01:17:56,976
Tell me!
Tell me quickly!

1338
01:17:57,063 --> 01:17:58,543
What were his
final words to you?

1339
01:17:58,630 --> 01:18:00,284
I don’t understand!

1340
01:18:01,720 --> 01:18:02,634
Telemachus...

1341
01:18:02,721 --> 01:18:05,681
What were his final words?

1342
01:18:05,768 --> 01:18:08,945
He said, third gift.

1343
01:18:10,468 --> 01:18:11,382
Third gift?

1344
01:18:13,427 --> 01:18:14,341
And then...

1345
01:18:15,778 --> 01:18:16,909
And then nothing more.

1346
01:18:22,219 --> 01:18:23,394
So clever.

1347
01:18:24,787 --> 01:18:26,702
The king of Ithaca.

1348
01:18:26,789 --> 01:18:28,529
Clever to his last.

1349
01:18:30,314 --> 01:18:31,228
Oh, goddess Athena.

1350
01:18:32,925 --> 01:18:34,405
My love.

1351
01:18:34,492 --> 01:18:39,192
You have suffered
enough these past many days.

1352
01:18:39,279 --> 01:18:41,629
But the game’s over.

1353
01:18:41,717 --> 01:18:44,067
Odysseus...

1354
01:18:44,154 --> 01:18:45,329
Wins.

1355
01:18:45,416 --> 01:18:47,157
What do you mean?

1356
01:18:47,244 --> 01:18:48,811
He didn’t win.

1357
01:18:48,898 --> 01:18:50,595
He’s dead.
He’s gone!

1358
01:18:50,682 --> 01:18:54,425
Do not be troubled,
son of Odysseus.

1359
01:18:54,512 --> 01:18:58,124
Once again, your
father’s glory honors me.

1360
01:19:00,300 --> 01:19:02,650
With his final breath,

1361
01:19:02,738 --> 01:19:06,306
your husband requested his
last wish.

1362
01:19:09,222 --> 01:19:11,094
May I grant it?

1363
01:19:11,181 --> 01:19:12,051
Yes.

1364
01:19:15,489 --> 01:19:16,403
Yes, please.

1365
01:19:19,929 --> 01:19:23,541
Clever is indeed
greater than good.

1366
01:19:38,034 --> 01:19:39,644
- Father!
- He’s alive!

1367
01:19:42,038 --> 01:19:43,779
Goddess Athena, thank you.

1368
01:19:43,866 --> 01:19:46,303
It was your husband
who outsmarted death.

1369
01:19:48,174 --> 01:19:50,916
I am glad to see
you all reunited.

1370
01:19:59,969 --> 01:20:01,753
My son...

1371
01:20:01,840 --> 01:20:02,754
Oh, my son.

1372
01:20:05,061 --> 01:20:05,931
I was gone.

1373
01:20:07,498 --> 01:20:08,455
The goddess.

1374
01:20:11,545 --> 01:20:15,723
Athena has granted me
my third wish.

1375
01:20:17,725 --> 01:20:20,511
My father more clever
than the gods themselves.

1376
01:20:27,997 --> 01:20:30,434
The river Styx can wait...

1377
01:20:33,698 --> 01:20:35,091
But a moment longer.

1378
01:20:36,266 --> 01:20:37,136
Oh...

1379
01:20:38,790 --> 01:20:40,096
You took your time, old man.

1380
01:20:41,967 --> 01:20:43,534
The wind.

1381
01:20:43,621 --> 01:20:44,752
The wind was against me.

1382
01:20:47,016 --> 01:20:50,193
I have heard such stories.

1383
01:20:53,979 --> 01:20:57,113
You seem as if from a dream.

1384
01:20:57,200 --> 01:20:59,115
Are you really here?

1385
01:20:59,202 --> 01:21:01,857
Or are the gods
still playing games.

1386
01:21:04,468 --> 01:21:09,865
I have not the strength
for their sport any longer.

1387
01:21:09,952 --> 01:21:11,127
Nor do I.

1388
01:21:11,214 --> 01:21:12,650
Nor do I, my queen.

1389
01:21:16,045 --> 01:21:19,570
King of Ithaca, why have
you returned to this place?

1390
01:21:25,532 --> 01:21:29,058
I have but one goal.

1391
01:21:29,145 --> 01:21:30,015
One destination.

1392
01:21:33,497 --> 01:21:37,718
Not Ithaca, not this house,

1393
01:21:37,805 --> 01:21:39,459
not the crown was my goal.

1394
01:21:43,028 --> 01:21:46,292
I care not for the seas
or the skies.

1395
01:21:48,512 --> 01:21:51,819
I sought no monsters, nor gods.

1396
01:21:54,735 --> 01:21:56,302
I only sought you.

1397
01:21:58,739 --> 01:22:00,132
Only you.

1398
01:22:00,219 --> 01:22:05,572
You... You were the
safe harbor I fought

1399
01:22:05,659 --> 01:22:09,054
so desperately to get home to.

1400
01:22:18,281 --> 01:22:19,978
And home you will stay.

1401
01:22:21,458 --> 01:22:22,328
Yes, I will.

1402
01:22:26,854 --> 01:22:27,812
Home.

1403
01:22:30,206 --> 01:22:32,338
The only journey
worth the fight.

1404
01:22:38,997 --> 01:22:40,781
Home.

1405
01:22:40,868 --> 01:22:42,218
Home at last.
